  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 AM EDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nToday-Tonight...A strong mid/upper level ridge over the plains\ndrifts southeastward today, as a trough dips through the\nNortheast. Locally, ample moisture, with PWATs 2-2.15\", and upper\nlevel perturbations will support scattered shower and \nthunderstorm development this afternoon. PoPs are forecast between\n30-50%, highest south of Orlando. Coverage looks to begin after 2\nPM, peaking in the late afternoon and early evening, before \nactivity drifts offshore and drier conditions prevail overnight. \nPrevailing SW flow around 10 mph will pin the east coast sea \nbreeze, which may locally enhance any storms. The main threats \ntoday will be lightning strikes and wind gusts up to 55 mph, \nthanks to DCAPE near 1000 J/kg and steep low level lapse rates. \nBut, slow storm motions and deep moisture could lead to locally \nheavy rainfall and minor flooding in low- lying and poor drainage \nareas.\n\nA Heat Advisory has been issued once again today for areas from\nOrange and northern Brevard Counties northward. Humid conditions\nand temperatures in the mid to upper 90s in this area are forecast\nto produce heat indices up to 109 degrees. A limiting factor will\nbe an earlier start to convection than forecast. However, mid-\nlevel cloud cover looks to be lower than yesterday, favoring \nwarmer conditions. Regardless, conditions will be hot and muggy\nacross east central Florida. Be sure to practice heat safety\ntoday. Know the signs of heat stroke and take action, if needed.\nVery warm conditions persist through tonight, with overnight lows\nin the mid to upper 70s.\n\nWednesday...The upper level ridge axis stretches eastward towards\nFlorida Wednesday, as a surface front slowly sags through the\nSoutheast. Slightly below normal PoPs are forecast under upper\nlevel convergence. However, deep moisture lingers over the\npeninsula, supporting scattered coverage in the afternoon and\nevening (30-40%). Dry air in the mid levels maintains DCAPE values\nnear 1100 J/kg and the threat for a few strong wind gusts near 55\nmph, in addition to lightning strikes and locally heavy rainfall.\nSW flow increases as the pressure gradient tightens between the\nfront and the Atlantic ridge axis south of the area. Winds\nincrease to near 15 mph Wednesday afternoon, with gusts 20-25 mph.\nLikely the hottest day of the week, highs reach the lower to\nmid-90s once again. Additional Heat Advisories will be possible,\nas heat indices reach 105-110 degrees.\n\nAs showers and storms drift offshore in the evening, drier\nconditions develop Wednesday night. However, will see isolated\nto scattered activity drift southward into areas north of I-4 into\nearly Thursday morning, as the front continues southward.\nOvernight lows may not dip below 80 in some locations Wednesday\nnight.\n\nThursday-Monday...Southwesterly flow prevails through most of the\nremainder of the forecast period, as the Atlantic ridge axis \nremains pinned the Straits of Florida. A low pressure system \nmoves offshore from the Northeast US Thursday, dragging a cold\nfront into the Southeast, where is it forecast to stall in the\nvicinity of north Florida. Support near the front and PWATs in\nexcess of 2\" will lead to above normal coverage of showers and \nstorms into the weekend. PoPs 50-70+% across the area will linger\ninto Sunday, before high pressure builds aloft early next week.\nWill need to monitor the threat for some locally heavy rainfall,\nwith multiple rounds of training showers and storms possible. Most\nof the forecast area has been highlighted in a Marginal Risk (1/4)\nfor excessive rainfall Thursday and Friday. Enough lingering drier\nair in the mid-levels could support a few strong wind gusts\nThursday, with peak gusts near 50 mph.\n\nHigh coverage of clouds and precipitation will give east central\nFlorida a break from the persistent higher heat indices. However,\nhighs are still forecast to reach the lower 90s each afternoon\ninto the weekend, creeping towards the mid-90s early next week. In\naddition, peak heat indices are still forecast to reach 100-105.\nBreezy SW flow with gusts 20-25 mph Thursday will diminish into\nthe weekend, as the pressure gradient relaxes slightly.",
